   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">From February 2020 to December 2021,  the Government of Nigeria operationalized the implementation of the Nigerian Youth Employment Action Plan (NIYEAP), that outlines four strategic lines of action: employability, entrepreneurship, employment and equality and rights. The Ministry established the implementation and monitoring mechanism for the NIYEAP in December 2021, as it developed a monitoring framework that will be used to review the implementation plan. In addition, the Ministry revitalized the National Youth Employment Action NIYEAP Committee, which has not met for about four years. The Committee, which met in October 2021, aims to review progress made, propose priority areas for action and ensure sustainability of results achieved. The ILO continued to support the Federal Ministry of Youth and Sports Development in advancing the Nigerian Youth Employment Action Plan in collaboration with the UN Department of Economic and Social Affairs (DESA). In the last quarter of 2020, a Youth Employment Inventory (YEI) was conducted among more than 40 key public and private youth employment stakeholders to identify relevant ongoing and upcoming youth employment programmes in Nigeria; main areas of intervention; result measurement needs; and the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on programme delivery. In June 2021, the ILO conducted a six-week online course for youth employment stakeholders in Nigeria on the monitoring and evaluation of youth employment programmes. The tailor-made course included plenary session with lectures on different topics, group-work, and self-study and was attended by some 45 stakeholders from government, social partners, civil society, the private sector and youth organizations. A total of 48 participants successfully completed the course. According to the course evaluation, almost 90 per cent were &quot;very satisfied&quot; with the course. The training is informing the development of the monitoring and evaluation framework of the NIYEAP.

To gather youth aspirations and perspectives on the world of work in Nigeria, the ILO supported a survey conducted by the Nigerian Youth SDGs Network that was administered online, face-to-face and through U-Report Nigeria and completed by more than 100,000 respondents. The Network also organized youth labs to raise awareness on the NIYEAP in each geopolitical zone in Nigeria.

In December 2020, the ILO conducted a training-of-trainers in the Start and Improve Your Business Programme to capacitate a total of ten representatives of the Federal Ministry of Youth and Sports Development engaged in the delivery of online training for young people in Nigeria. Trained trainers have been certified as SIYB trainers in September 2021.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.1.2. Number of member States with a national strategy for youth employment, as a distinct strategy or as part of a national employment strategy (based on SDG indicator 8.b.1)</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.3. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement policies for a just transition towards environmentally sustainable economies and societies</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Nigerian Government, through the Federal Ministry of Environment (Department of Climate Change) and the Federal Ministry of Labour and Employment, has developed a National Climate Change Policy for Nigeria 2021 &#x2013; 2030 by July 2021. 
This took place within the context of Nigeria&apos;s National Determined Climate Contributions (NDC). Employment and Just Transition Policies and Green Jobs promotion were included in Nigeria&apos;s policy responses to Climate Change. The ILO built the capacity of 50 national actors on the impact of climate change and climate policies on social and employment outcomes and just transition policies, through a physical inception meeting, held in March 2020. The workshop  featured social dialogue among social partners and line ministries and a training on just transition policies and the Green Jobs Assessment Model (GJAM) in collaboration with the UNDP. A research team was set up and a GJAM was conducted. Follow-up consultations were held with stakeholders through online meetings over the year 2020 and 2021. The model assessed the impact of  11 climate policies on the labour market, and evidence-based policy advice derived was validated in a final social dialogue meeting.</narrative>
   </description>
